"not good at taking criticism\" maybe from Agent 1 notes. But we have only the listed facts.\n\n- Candidate B: very conscientious; handles stress very well; good at assessing weather conditions; excellent computer skills. Weak memory for numbers; makes nasty remarks about colleagues; pretentious; wrong tone.\n\n- Candidate C: can make correct decisions quickly; keeps calm in a crisis; understands complicated technology; difficulty communicating ideas; regarded as egocentric; not very willing to further his education. Also from Agent 3: puts concern for others above everything; excellent attention skills.\n\n- Candidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately; can concentrate very well; solves problems extremely well; takes responsibility seriously. Regarded as a know-it-all; has a hot temper; moody; loner; arrogance; weak leadership.\n\nWe need to evaluate criteria: reliability, technical competence, calmness under pressure, cooperation, responsibility, etc.\n\nWe need to bring in missing criteria: crew cooperation, professional communication, attention & information accuracy, adaptability/feedback orientation.\n\nWe have some facts: Candidate C has excellent attention skills (attention & info accuracy). Candidate D can concentrate very well (also attention). Candidate B has weak memory for numbers (attention). Candidate A unknown.\n\nCrew cooperation: Candidate C puts concern for others above everything (positive). Candidate B makes nasty remarks (negative). Candidate D is a loner, moody, hot temper (negative). Candidate A is regarded as a show-off, not open to new ideas (negative). So C seems best for cooperation.\n\nProfessional communication: Candidate B makes nasty remarks, pretentious, wrong tone (negative). Candidate A show-off, not open to new ideas (negative). Candidate D arrogance, know-it-all (negative). Candidate C difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric (negative). All have issues, but C's concern for others may mitigate.\n\nAdaptability/feedback